he center of the fire. The wind is blowing toward 205, so a heading of 25 pushes against it and slows the fall. I'll simulate with no updraft to find a release point that gives an impact near the origin."}, {"before_call": 10, "text": "The release point at (-13, -29) puts the drop on the center without updraft. With the updraft it should lift and carry farther down-range, so I'll run the test drop to see the actual behavior."}, {"before_call": 11, "text": "The fall took 4.32 seconds against a predicted 3.82, so something is pushing the object up. I'll check that against the updraft values."}, {"before_call": 12, "text": "Updraft 3 gives dt 0, and the dx of -3.3 is within noise, so it seems fine. I'm thinking about checking 2.5 and 3.5 to gauge sensitivity, since dt might shift by about 0.1 s per 0.2 of updraft. I'll try 3.3 next."}, {"before_call": 13, "text": "I'm finding the updraft sits near 3.0 with some uncertainty, and the stronger hotspots could push it toward 4.2 m/s, though that's hard to see through the smoke. I want drops at different geometry to pin it down, with five test drops left. I'm also checking whether the dx offset in test 5 points to a small error in another parameter, and planning a more discriminating test next.\n\nI'm weighing a high-altitude drop at speed 45 so the descent runs through the smoke, since the wind seems to drift. Across the altitude tests the wind direction shifts a little with height, with a possible veer of roughly -4 degrees per 100 m, though that estimate is shaky given the 4-degree noise. Wind speed also falls off with height, which fits a modest shear. For the remaining drops I'm considering low and high altitudes at speed 80 on east and west headings, to refine the estimates and detect the veer."}, {"before_call": 16, "text": "The high-altitude points both come out a bit too long, so the model probably needs slightly less drag or more shear. I'll try drag at 0.027 and shear at 0.25."}, {"before_call": 18, "text": "The residual noise sits around 1.5, which seems good enough for now. Test 6 shows my prediction landing too far north, since the observed drift was more southward at 150 m, so I'm considering a veer of +2 or a higher shear of 0.25 to account for that."}, {"before_call": 20, "text": "The mean x-offset is biased negative, around -1.5, so the predicted positions sit a bit west of the observations. That suggests the wind direction needs a small shift: nudging it from 205 toward 180 moves the drift eastward, so I'll try about 203 with the speed at 10.9."}, {"before_call": 21, "text": "I've settled on the drag, wind, shear, and updraft values for the model. The remaining three drops could refine the updraft estimate, since the calibration fire only covers intensity 1. I'm considering a slow pass through the center at 150 m and one more low-altitude smoke test at 30 m.\n\nI'm checking the updraft effect near the ground: it scales with altitude up to 15 m, so it's mostly at full strength here. I'll try a high-altitude smoke drop at 150 m and 45 m/s, where the payload should spend a long time in the column, and use the simulator to find a release point that hits the center with updraft 3."}, {"before_call": 22, "text": "I'm noticing the impact comes after the release point. Releasing at (16, 38) looks like it lands near the center, though I might still be in the smoke at the end. I'll go with a release at (16, 38.5)."}, {"before_call": 23, "text": "I'm comparing the observed fall time of 10.0 with the predicted 11.01 for updraft 3. The observed value is shorter, and with no updraft at 150 the fall time is about 9.7, so the updraft in the final part may be weaker than 3. I want to check several updraft values to see which one fits."}, {"before_call": 25, "text": "The simulation's fall time came out at 11 against an observed 10, but that was for a different release point, so the gap is explained by where the smoke was released. The comparison still holds up: the updraft of 3 fits the data with only a small time difference. I'll estimate the updraft is somewhere between 3.0 and 3.3, and try 3.5 to bracket the upper end."}, {"before_call": 26, "text": "I'm checking that the updraft is around 3.2, which is fine. I'm weighing whether to use the two spare test drops, but they aren't needed, so I'm moving on to begin operation."}, {"before_call": 27, "text": "I'm picking mid-range parameters for each target and simulating a release from (0,0) to measure the offset, then I'll correct for it. The offset barely changes with location, except where the path crosses smoke. I'm also wondering whether the simulator only places the fire at the origin, since that would make the smoke calibration meaningless for other release points.\n\nI'm checking whether the simulator only models the calibration fire at the origin, since that decides how I can account for smoke at the real target. The smoke parameters can't be set per fire in the simulator, so I'm considering simulating with no updraft away from the origin and estimating the smoke effect separately.\n\nI'm weighing a simulation with the updraft scaled to each target's intensity and the radius left at 50, which would overstate the smoke effect but is simple to run. Since I'm aiming for the target centre, the load's final descent is the part that sits in the column, so what matters is how long it spends there.\n\nNext I'm taking target 1 (intensity about 1.38), using an updraft of 4.4 in place of the scaled 3.2\u00d71.38 and keeping the radius at 50. I'll run the first simulation with the release at (0,0), with heading 62, altitude 81 and speed 56."}, {"before_call": 29, "text": "I'm noticing the smoke effect is small because the release sits right at the origin, so the simulation shows smoke at the start instead of the end. I'll move the release point to about (-40, 15) so the impact lands at the origin and the load shows up in the smoke at the end, and I'll run that for updraft 4.4 at (-40.4, 15.6)."}, {"before_call": 30, "text": "I'm testing a release point at (-38, 20) to see if it lands near the origin, and checking how the load behaves relative to the smoke boundary at that distance. The exact outcome seems to hinge on whether the simulation's smoke geometry matches the target's, since the target range of 58.7 is larger than the 50 I'd been using.\n\nI'm thinking the real effect is somewhat larger, so I'll take the (-38, 20) release and check it."}, {"before_call": 31, "text": "I'm offsetting the release point by (-38, 20), which puts it at (-943, 549). The updraft's effect looks modest, shifting the impact by roughly 5 m. I'm wondering how that compares to the case with no updraft from the same release point.\n\nI'm checking the updraft's influence on the impact point, which comes out to about 8 m from the no-updraft case, and a 10% change in the updraft strength would shift it by less than 1 m. The radius mismatch might add around 2 m of error. I'll run all the simulations before the drops, since the drops don't depend on them, and next I'm estimating the release offset for target 2 with its updraft of about 1.7."}, {"before_call": 32, "text": "I'm checking the impact point against the offset from the starting position, which comes out to (43, -82). The release point for a target at the origin would be around (-43, 82), but that sits about 92 units from the center, so the load probably only enters the smoke near the end. The smoke radius here is 36.7 against 50 in the simulation, so I'll run it with updraft set to 0 to see how big the smoke effect is."}, {"before_call": 34, "text": "I've confirmed the smoke effect is tiny, around 2 m, and I'm settling on a release point for target 2 at about (-869, -1615). For target 3 I'm taking the parameters as given: updraft 1.9 with release at the origin, skipping a no-updraft pass."}, {"before_call": 36, "text": "I'm checking the impact position against the launch point: from (-50, 0) to (-7.7, 9.3) is only about 42 m eas